course: dictionary
lesson: 1304
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Cabin» can mean a small wooden house, a small room on a ship, or the part of a plane where passengers sit. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: «The family spent the week in a log cabin by the lake.» Log, lake and the week away tell you this is a wooden house, not a room on a ship. Ask yourself what the word means here, then check that your meaning still makes sense in the sentence.
explain: Some words keep their meaning inside a phrase. «The cabin crew asked everyone to fasten their seat belts.» Cabin crew means the flight attendants on a plane. Other phrases like cabin fever and cabin bag work the same way: the whole phrase has its own meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: One word can have several meanings: «cabin» can mean a wooden house, a small room on a ship, or the part of a plane where passengers sit.
fact: Cabin crew are the flight attendants on a plane, who look after passengers and explain what to do in an emergency.
fact: Cabin fever means feeling trapped indoors for too long.
fact: On a narrowboat, many boat families lived in a tiny cabin at the back, and the boats were only about 2 metres wide.
exercise: «The family spent the week in a log cabin by the lake.» What kind of cabin is this? | *A small wooden house in the woods | The part of a plane where passengers sit | A small room for sleeping on a ship
exercise: «The cabin crew asked everyone to fasten their seat belts.» Who are the cabin crew? | The sailors who sleep on a ship | The people who build log houses | *The flight attendants on a plane | The pilots who fly the plane
exercise: «He slept in the small cabin below deck, next to the captain.» Which meaning fits best? | A wooden house in the country | The flight attendants on a plane | A lift that never stops | *A small room for sleeping on a ship
test: Priya says «cabin» only means a house in the woods. Why is she wrong? | *Cabin also means a small room on a ship and the part of a plane where passengers sit | Cabin only means a wooden house, and nothing else | Cabin means a very loud noise | Cabin means the front wheel of a car
test: «Cabin fever» is a phrase. What does it mean? | Being too tall to fit in a cabin | Getting lost in the mountains | Feeling cold in a wooden house | *Feeling trapped indoors for too long
exam: Which sentence uses cabin to mean a small room on a ship? | «The cabin crew served us drinks on the flight.» | *«The captain's cabin's good enough for me,» said the Sea Rat. | «The cabin was painted with roses and castles.» | «We rented a cabin by the lake for the summer.»
